Mesenchymal tumors associated with osteomalacia oncog√©nica can be found in several anatomical locations, including long bones, distal extremities, the inguinal region, nasopharynx, and nasal sinuses. The diverse locations of these tumors can lead to a range of clinical presentations, which may delay diagnosis and treatment. Understanding these locations is vital for clinicians to consider when evaluating patients with symptoms of osteomalacia oncog√©nica. [Data: Entities (5725, 5724, 5728, 5726, 5727); Relationships (15136, 15135, 15139, 15137, 15138)]

