The relationships among Metformina, Ranitidina, and Zidovudina illustrate the interconnectedness of medications in patient care. This network of relationships highlights the importance of considering multiple medications when developing treatment plans for patients with complex health needs. The collaborative use of these medications can enhance treatment efficacy and improve patient outcomes, particularly in those with overlapping health issues. [Data: Relationships (13582, 13583)]# ASPEN and Clinical Nutrition Community

The community is centered around the American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ASPEN) and its relationships with various organizations and fields related to clinical nutrition. ASPEN plays a pivotal role in establishing guidelines for nutritional support in critically ill patients, collaborating with entities like the Canadian Society for Clinical Nutrition (CSCN) and the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN).

## ASPEN's central role in clinical nutrition guidelines

ASPEN is a leading organization in the field of clinical nutrition, providing comprehensive guidelines for both parenteral and enteral nutrition. Its guidelines are essential for healthcare providers managing the nutritional needs of patients, particularly those in critical care settings. ASPEN's emphasis on evidence-based practices ensures that patients receive appropriate nutritional support tailored to their medical conditions, which is crucial for recovery and overall health. The organization has a long history of developing clinical practice guidelines, with its first publication dating back to 1993, highlighting its established authority in the field. [Data: Entities (603); Relationships (4335, 2941, 4351, 4354, 4360, +more)]

## Collaboration with international organizations

ASPEN collaborates with several international organizations, including ESPEN and CSCN, to enhance the quality of nutritional care globally. This collaboration allows for the sharing of best practices and the establishment of international standards for nutritional support. For instance, both ASPEN and ESPEN work together to publish evidence-based guidelines that address the nutritional needs of various patient populations, including those undergoing surgery and oncology patients. This partnership is vital for ensuring that healthcare providers have access to the most current and effective nutritional practices. [Data: Relationships (2941, 4350, 4360, 4356, 4363, +more)]

## Focus on critically ill patients

